            <ArticleTitle>Does adolescent obesity affect surgical presentation and radiographic outcome for patients with adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>53-58</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The purpose of this study was to test a hypothesis that overweight patients with adolescent idiopathic scoliosis present with larger curves and achieve less surgical correction than do healthy weight counterparts. A total of 251 individuals were grouped by BMI into overweight (BMI% ≥85) and healthy weight (BMI% &lt;85) groups. Overweight patients demonstrated significantly larger intraoperative blood loss (P=0.041), although there was no significant difference in the number of intraoperative transfusions. Major curves and surgical correction were similar between the two groups. A greater postoperative thoracic kyphosis at latest follow-up may suggest a worsening sagittal profile in these individuals postoperatively.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
